1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,560
That lower car payment? It just cost you over five thousand dollars.

2
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:08,200
Say you borrow about forty-four thousand seven hundred dollars for a new car.

3
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:10,980
That's the average new car loan, says Edmunds.

4
00:00:11,300 --> 00:00:14,900
The bank charges seven percent a year in interest, the fee for borrowing.

5
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:18,320
Now pick: pay it off in four years, or seven?

6
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:21,800
Four years means about one thousand seventy dollars a month.

7
00:00:22,360 --> 00:00:23,700
Seven years? Only about six hundred— six hundred seventy-four.

8
00:00:26,060 --> 00:00:28,120
Hit like if you'd grab the seven-year deal.

9
00:00:28,120 --> 00:00:31,440
That's three hundred ninety-five bucks back every month.

10
00:00:32,020 --> 00:00:35,520
But here's the catch: interest piles up every month you owe.

11
00:00:36,220 --> 00:00:39,220
Four years costs about six thousand seven hundred in interest.

12
00:00:39,820 --> 00:00:41,000
Seven years? Almost twelve thousand dollars.

13
00:00:42,980 --> 00:00:45,420
Send this to the friend bragging about a low payment.

14
00:00:46,040 --> 00:00:49,480
Same car, same rate, about five thousand three hundred more.

15
00:00:49,940 --> 00:00:53,460
At the typical wage, that's two hundred sixteen hours of work.

16
00:00:53,900 --> 00:00:56,860
More than five full work weeks, just for the longer loan.

17
00:00:57,380 --> 00:00:59,960
Follow if you like seeing the bill behind the price tag.

18
00:01:00,440 --> 00:01:03,100
And when it's paid off, your car is seven years old.

19
00:01:03,540 --> 00:01:05,980
So the payment went down, but the price went up.

20
00:01:06,520 --> 00:01:08,160
What's your car loan term, in years?

